This Chapter explains open source software, how it differs from proprietary software, and the importance of software licensing.

Open source software is software where the source code is available for people to view, use, modify and share, depending on the licence. The chapter introduces organisations such as the Free Software Foundation (FSF) and the Open Source Initiative (OSI), which have played important roles in promoting and defining principles around free and open source software.

The chapter also explains that software can have different types of licences, and users need to understand the conditions attached to those licences. It introduces concepts such as copyright, licensing, redistribution and modification.

It also discusses standards organisations such as IEEE and POSIX, which help establish standards that improve compatibility and consistency between operating systems and software

What I Have Learned

From this chapter, I have learned what open source software means and how it differs from proprietary software. I now understand that having access to source code allows people to study, modify and share software, depending on the licence.

I have also learned that open source software still has licensing requirements, so it is important to understand what you are allowed to do with the software.

I learned about the role of organisations such as the FSF and OSI in promoting software freedom and open source principles. I also learned about IEEE and POSIX and how standards help different systems and software work consistently together.

Overall, this chapter helped me understand that using software isn't just about installing and running it; it's also important to understand its licence, permissions and responsibilities.
